Pilot of the stack conversion (CLAUDE.md, first rule; background in .claude/rules/sick-rules.md, hazards). The distribution was built on "core Perl plus YAML::PP, plain bless", a rule the maintainer never gave. The target is the house stack: Moo, Path::Tiny, JSON::MaybeXS. This card converts the smallest class and sets the pattern the following cards copy.

SICK::Device (154 lines): plain bless, read-only accessors, spec, path.

Done when:
- SICK::Device is a Moo class per skill getty-perl-moo: ro attributes, no hand-written new.
- Behaviour is unchanged: names still returns a copy, spec and path as before, the same constructor arguments, the same croak messages (t/13-config.t pins them). A message that has to change is named in the report.
- cpanfile names Moo and whatever else is loaded now.
- The claim "core Perl and YAML::PP" is rewritten to what is true after this card: README.md (the intro, and the paragraph on device types that need "more than core Perl"), lib/SICK.pm (POD and its dependency list), the bullet in Changes.
- docker/Dockerfile: check how the image gets its Perl dependencies. If it lists modules by hand, add the new ones. Not runnable on this machine: report it as unverified.
- prove -lr t green; every file that ended in skip_all is named.

Not in this card: SICK::Command, SICK::OpenSSL, SICK::Names, SICK::Duration and SICK::Decision are function modules without file access. They stay as they are.

Device is Moo (ro attributes, BUILDARGS keeps the croak messages, names a copy via _names); cpanfile Moo + namespace::autoclean 0.16; Dockerfile installs libmoo-perl, libnamespace-autoclean-perl; dependency claim rewritten in README, SICK.pm, Changes. prove -lr t, Cisco suite, dzil test green; t/e2e/run-local.sh ALL PASSED. Tree commit-ready, uncommitted.

